    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.intersection - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.intersection.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.intersection()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Compute the intersection of this graph with another graph.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.intersection()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Compute the intersection of this graph with another graph.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.intersection()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Compute the intersection of this graph with another graph.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCompute the intersection of this graph with another graph.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L49437-L49553){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.intersection(other)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Intersection](../assets/graphs/intersection.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"G and H share edges A–B and B–D; A–C is only in G (accent) and C–D only in H (teal). The intersection keeps just the shared edges A–B and B–D; A–C and C–D are dashed, and node C, left with no edges, is dropped too.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nReturns a new graph containing only edges that exist in both graphs. Nodes are included only\nif they are endpoints of common edges.\n\nEdges are matched by their endpoint node IDs: an edge counts as shared when both graphs\nconnect the same pair of IDs (in the same direction, for directed graphs). Weights and\nattributes play no part in the matching, so an edge with weight `1.0` in one graph and\n`3.0` in the other is still shared. Intersection answers \"which connections are present\neverywhere?\", which is useful for:\n\n- finding the stable core of a network observed at several points in time\n- keeping only interactions confirmed by two independent data sources or experiments\n- comparing two inferred networks (e.g., from different methods) for agreement\n\nBecause nodes are matched by ID, both graphs must use the same numbering for the same\nentities. Nodes that are not part of any shared edge are dropped, even if both graphs\ncontain them. Edges keep this graph's IDs. Neither input graph is modified.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[other]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The graph to intersect with. It must have the same directedness as this graph. Only\n    its edge set (by endpoint IDs) decides which edges survive; for surviving edges and\n    nodes, its labels, attributes, and weights are used only where this graph has no\n    value of its own.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   A new graph containing edges present in both graphs, plus their endpoints. It is\n    weighted if either input is weighted.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If the graphs have different directedness.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nIntersection keeps only edges present in both graphs and drops any node that would\nend up isolated as a result, matching the algebraic identity\n$E(G_1 \\cap G_2) = E_1 \\cap E_2$ followed by node pruning. Both operands must share\nthe same directedness. Where both graphs carry a weight, label, or attribute for the\nsame edge or node, the calling graph's value wins and the other graph fills in only\nwhat the calling graph lacks, which is worth knowing when weights differ. A common\nuse is identifying the stable core of a network observed at multiple time points.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~difference`) \n\n:   Keep the edges that the other graph lacks.\n\n[](`~symmetric_difference`) \n\n:   Keep the edges in exactly one of two graphs.\n\n[](`~intersection_all`) \n\n:   Keep the edges common to any number of graphs.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nOnly the edges in both graphs are kept. A triangle and a square share the edges `1--2` and\n`2--3`. Node `4` is only in the square, so it's dropped:\n\n::: {#da8d7a91 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ntriangle = ec.Graph().add_cycle_graph(3)\nsquare = ec.Graph().add_cycle_graph(4)\n\ncommon = triangle.intersection(square)\ncommon.get_edges_str_repr(), sorted(common.get_node_ids())\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(['1--2', '2--3'], [1, 2, 3])\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Friendships that lasted\n\n`ec.friends()` is a network of friendships in 2024, and `snapshot=2` gives the same people\na year later, with 8 new friendships. Three of the 2025 friendships then ended in 2026.\nThe snapshots share their node IDs, so the intersection of 2024 and 2026 holds the\nfriendships that lasted, 40 of the 43:\n\n::: {#58936805 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ny2024 = ec.friends()\ny2026 = (\n    ec.friends(snapshot=2)\n    .delete_edge(\"Dev\", \"Femi\")\n    .delete_edge(\"Gwen\", \"Lou\")\n    .delete_edge(\"Rosa\", \"Sven\")\n)\n\nlasted = y2024.intersection(y2026)\nlasted.count_nodes(), lasted.count_edges()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(24, 40)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nIn the 2024 network, the friendships that lasted are highlighted. The three faint ones\nended: Dev and Femi, Gwen and Lou, and Rosa and Sven:\n\n::: {#51dc2037 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ny2024.plot(\n    color_by=\"club\", label_by=\"label\", seed=2,\n    highlight_edges=[(s, t) for _, s, t in lasted.get_edges_as_tuples()],\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-the